---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * Written against UK GDPR Articles 12 to 14 (as amended by the Data (Use and Access) Act 2025),
 * section 164A of the Data Protection Act 2018 (complaints to the controller, in force
 * 19 June 2026), and PECR Schedule A1 (storage and access technologies, in force 5 February 2026).
 *
 * Every factual statement here describes how this codebase actually behaves. If the code,
 * providers or retention periods change, this page must change in the same commit.
 */
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { business } from '../config/business';

const updated = '10 November 2026';
const sections = [
  { id: 'summary', label: 'Summary' },
  { id: 'who-we-are', label: 'Who we are' },
  { id: 'scope', label: 'What this policy covers' },
  { id: 'information', label: 'Information we collect' },
  { id: 'use', label: 'How and why we use it' },
  { id: 'sharing', label: 'Who we share it with' },
  { id: 'transfers', label: 'Transfers outside the UK' },
  { id: 'retention', label: 'How long we keep it' },
  { id: 'rights', label: 'Your rights' },
  { id: 'complaints', label: 'Complaints' },
  { id: 'cookies', label: 'Cookies' },
  { id: 'security', label: 'Security' },
  { id: 'other', label: 'Children, links and changes' },
];
const phoneHref = business.telephone.replace(/[^\d+]/g, '');
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="How Presence Web Services collects, uses, shares and protects personal information, how long it is kept, and the rights you have over it."
  sections={sections}
  updated={updated}
>
  <section id="summary" class="legal-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-heading">
    <h2 id="summary-heading">Summary</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        We collect what you give us when you request an audit or contact us, and use it to reply,
        carry out the audit and, if you want, provide our services.
      </li>
      <li>
        We don’t sell your information or use it for advertising. We may phone, visit or, where the
        law allows, email businesses to offer our services, and we stop as soon as you ask.
      </li>
      <li>
        This website uses no analytics, advertising or tracking cookies. The only cookie we set is a
        ten-minute cookie that confirms your request was received.
      </li>
      <li>
        Website enquiries are deleted automatically after 12 months unless they lead to paid work.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can ask to see, correct or delete your information, or complain, at any time. Details
        are below.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="who-we-are">
    <h2>1. Who we are</h2>
    <p>
      Presence Web Services is the trading name of {business.legalName || 'its owner'}, a sole
      trader. In this policy, “we”, “us” and “our” mean {business.legalName || 'the owner'} trading
      as Presence Web Services. We are the controller of the personal information described here,
      which means we decide how and why it is used and are responsible for protecting it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {business.correspondenceAddress && <li>Address: {business.correspondenceAddress}</li>}
      <li>
        Email: <a href={`mailto:${business.email}`}>{business.email}</a>
      </li>
      {business.telephone && (
        <li>
          Telephone: <a href={`tel:${phoneHref}`}>{business.telephone}</a>
        </li>
      )}
      {business.icoRegistration && (
        <li>Information Commission registration number: {business.icoRegistration}</li>
      )}
    </ul>
    <p>
      We are a one-person business and are not required to appoint a data protection officer.{' '}
      {business.founder.name} is responsible for how personal information is handled and is the
      person you will deal with about any privacy question.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scope">
    <h2>2. What this policy covers</h2>
    <p>This policy explains how we handle personal information about:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>people who visit this website;</li>
      <li>people who request a free audit or contact us by form, email or phone;</li>
      <li>
        people whose information appears in public material we review during an audit, such as a
        business website or Google Business Profile;
      </li>
      <li>
        people at businesses we contact to offer our services, by phone, in person or by email; and
      </li>
      <li>the contacts at businesses we quote for or work with.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      When we work on a client’s website or accounts, we may handle personal information about that
      client’s own customers, such as messages sent through their contact form. In that case the
      client is the controller and we act as their processor, following their instructions under the
      data processing terms in our <a href="/terms/#data-processing">terms of service</a>. The
      client’s own privacy notice applies to that information, not this one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="information">
    <h2>3. Information we collect</h2>
    <h3>Information you give us</h3>
    <p>When you request an audit through the form on this website, we ask for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>your name, your business name, your email address and your phone number (required);</li>
      <li>what you would like to improve (required);</li>
      <li>
        your website address, a link to your Google listing, your town or area, and the kind of help
        you are interested in (optional).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We need the required details to reply to you and carry out the audit. If you don’t provide
      them, we can’t process the request through the form, but you can still email us. You are not
      under any legal or contractual obligation to give us this information.
    </p>
    <p>
      We also keep what you tell us in emails, phone calls and meetings, and any information you
      give us so that we can quote for or carry out work, such as access details for your website or
      Google account, content and photographs, and your invoicing details.
    </p>
    <p>
      Please don’t send us passwords, identity documents or sensitive information, such as details
      about health, ethnicity, religion, politics, trade union membership, sexuality or criminal
      records. We don’t ask for it and don’t need it.
    </p>

    <h3>Information collected automatically</h3>
    <p>
      This website does not use analytics or tracking. When you use it, our hosting providers
      automatically process technical information needed to deliver and protect the website, such as
      your IP address, browser type and the pages requested. We don’t use this to identify you.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you submit the form, the website turns your IP address into a scrambled code using a
      secret key, and uses that code to limit how many requests can be sent in a short time. It does
      the same with your email address to limit how many confirmation emails one address can
      receive. Your actual IP address is not stored, and these codes are deleted once they are more
      than 24 hours old (see section 7).
    </p>

    <h3>Information from public sources</h3>
    <p>
      When we carry out an audit, we look at information you point us to and information that is
      publicly available about your business, such as your website, your Google Business Profile,
      online reviews, business directories and search results. This can include personal
      information, for example the name of a sole trader, staff names on a website or the names of
      people who have left reviews. We only use it to assess how your business appears online and to
      advise you.
    </p>
    <p>
      To find businesses we might be able to help, we also collect business contact details that
      businesses publish themselves, such as the business name, the owner’s or manager’s name, and
      the phone number and email address shown on the business’s website, Google Business Profile or
      directory listings. We keep brief notes of any conversation, including whether you are
      interested and whether you have asked us not to contact you again.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="use">
    <h2>4. How and why we use your information</h2>
    <p>
      Data protection law requires us to have a lawful basis for each use of personal information.
      These are the purposes we use it for and the basis we rely on.
    </p>
    <div class="legal-table" role="region" aria-label="Purposes and lawful bases" tabindex="0">
      <table role="table">
        <thead role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">
              What we use it for
            </th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">
              Information used
            </th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">
              Lawful basis
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Replying to your enquiry and carrying out the free audit
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Information used">
              Form details, correspondence, public information about your business
            </td>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Lawful basis">
              Taking steps at your request before entering into a contract, where you are enquiring
              for yourself, for example as a sole trader (Article 6(1)(b)). Otherwise, our
              legitimate interests in responding to business enquiries (Article 6(1)(f)).
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Emailing you to confirm we received your request
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Information used">
              Email address
            </td>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Lawful basis">
              Legitimate interests in confirming receipt and setting expectations (6(1)(f))
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Preparing a quote and providing our services
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Information used">
              Contact details, project information, access details, correspondence
            </td>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Lawful basis">
              Performing our contract with you (6(1)(b)), or, where our client is your employer or
              organisation, our legitimate interests in working with them (6(1)(f))
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Contacting businesses to offer our services, and keeping a record of anyone who has
              asked us not to contact them
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Information used">
              Business name, contact name, published business phone number and email address, public
              information about the business, notes of our contact
            </td>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Lawful basis">
              Legitimate interests in promoting our services to businesses, which the law recognises
              as an example of a legitimate interest (6(1)(f)). Keeping a do-not-contact record is
              also needed to comply with the Privacy and Electronic Communications Regulations
              (6(1)(c)).
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Invoicing, accounts and tax
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Information used">
              Name, business and contact details, invoices and payments
            </td>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Lawful basis">
              Compliance with our legal obligations, including tax law (6(1)(c))
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Keeping a record of enquiries and following up on a request you made
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Information used">
              Form details and correspondence
            </td>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Lawful basis">
              Legitimate interests in keeping accurate records of what was asked and agreed
              (6(1)(f))
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Protecting the website and preventing spam and abuse
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Information used">
              Scrambled IP and email codes, technical information
            </td>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Lawful basis">
              Legitimate interests in keeping the website and our systems secure, which the law
              recognises as an example of a legitimate interest (6(1)(f))
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Establishing, exercising or defending legal claims
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Information used">
              Any relevant records
            </td>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Lawful basis">
              Legitimate interests in protecting our legal position (6(1)(f))
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Responding to requests from regulators, courts or law enforcement
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Information used">
              Any relevant records
            </td>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Lawful basis">
              Compliance with a legal obligation (6(1)(c))
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Where we rely on legitimate interests, we have considered whether our use is necessary and
      whether it is outweighed by your interests and rights. You can ask us for more detail about
      that assessment, and you have the right to object (see section 8).
    </p>
    <h3>What we don’t do</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>We don’t sell or rent personal information, or share it for anyone else’s marketing.</li>
      <li>
        We don’t add you to a mailing list or send newsletters. When we contact a business to offer
        our services, we follow the Privacy and Electronic Communications Regulations: we check
        phone numbers against the Telephone Preference Service and Corporate Telephone Preference
        Service registers before calling, unless you have told us we may call; we never hide our
        number; we only email sole traders and partnerships with their consent; and every marketing
        email we send includes a simple way to opt out.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you ask us not to contact you again, by phone, email or in person, we stop straight away
        and add your business to our do-not-contact record.
      </li>
      <li>
        We don’t make decisions about you based solely on automated processing, including profiling,
        that have legal or similarly significant effects.
      </li>
      <li>
        We may use AI-assisted tools to help analyse public information during an audit or to help
        draft and review work. We don’t enter your contact details, or confidential information or
        personal data you give us, into those tools without your agreement, and a person reviews
        everything we send you.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If we want to use your information for a new purpose that isn’t compatible with the purpose we
      collected it for, we will tell you before we do.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sharing">
    <h2>5. Who we share it with</h2>
    <p>
      We use a small number of service providers to run this website and our email. They process
      personal information on our behalf, under contracts that require them to keep it secure and
      use it only as we instruct:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>OpenAI</strong>, which provides the platform this website is built and hosted on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong>, which provides the hosting network, the database that stores
        website enquiries, security protection and the forwarding of email sent to our address.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Resend</strong> (Plus Five Five, Inc.), which sends the email we receive about your
        request and the confirmation email we send to you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google</strong>, which provides the Gmail inbox where our email is kept.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>We may also share information:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        with professional advisers, such as an accountant or solicitor, who are bound by duties of
        confidentiality;
      </li>
      <li>
        with a subcontractor helping with your project, only if you have agreed to it in advance and
        under a written contract that protects your information;
      </li>
      <li>
        with HM Revenue and Customs, regulators, courts or law enforcement where the law requires
        it, or where it is needed to establish, exercise or defend legal claims; and
      </li>
      <li>
        with a buyer or successor if the business is ever sold or transferred, who would have to use
        it in line with this policy.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="transfers">
    <h2>6. Transfers outside the UK</h2>
    <p>
      Some of our service providers are based in, or use servers in, the United States and other
      countries outside the UK. When personal information is transferred outside the UK, it is
      protected in one of the ways UK data protection law allows:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        the country or organisation is covered by UK adequacy regulations, including the UK
        Extension to the EU-US Data Privacy Framework (sometimes called the UK-US data bridge) for
        US organisations certified under it; or
      </li>
      <li>
        the transfer is covered by the UK’s approved contract terms, either the International Data
        Transfer Agreement or the UK Addendum to the EU Standard Contractual Clauses, included in
        the provider’s data processing terms.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For example, Resend’s data processing terms state that its main processing takes place in the
      United States and that UK transfers are made under the UK Addendum to the EU Standard
      Contractual Clauses, and Resend states that it is certified under the Data Privacy Framework.
      You can ask us for more information about the safeguard used for any provider, and for a copy
      of it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="retention">
    <h2>7. How long we keep it</h2>
    <p>
      We keep personal information only for as long as we need it for the purposes above, and then
      delete it.
    </p>
    <div class="legal-table" role="region" aria-label="Retention periods" tabindex="0">
      <table role="table">
        <thead role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">
              Information
            </th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">
              How long we keep it
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Enquiries stored by the website
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long we keep it">
              12 months from when you send them, then deleted by the website’s automatic clean-up.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Emails and notes about an enquiry that doesn’t lead to paid work
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long we keep it">
              Deleted within 12 months of our last contact with you.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Business contact details and notes from contacting businesses
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long we keep it">
              Deleted within 12 months of our last contact if it doesn’t lead to an enquiry or paid
              work.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Do-not-contact record
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long we keep it">
              For as long as we contact businesses, so that we never contact you again by mistake.
              We keep only the business name, the number or address you asked us not to use, and the
              date.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Client records, contracts and project correspondence
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long we keep it">
              Six years after our work together ends, which is the time limit for most contract
              claims in Northern Ireland.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Invoices, payments and other accounting records
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long we keep it">
              At least five years after the 31 January filing deadline for the tax year they relate
              to, as HM Revenue and Customs requires, and in any case no less than the six years
              above.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Scrambled IP and email codes used to prevent abuse
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long we keep it">
              Deleted by the automatic clean-up once they are more than 24 hours old.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Request confirmation cookie
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long we keep it">
              Ten minutes.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    {business.privacyRetention && <p>{business.privacyRetention}</p>}
    <p>
      The website’s automatic clean-up runs whenever a request is submitted and whenever we review
      enquiries, and we review them regularly, so expired records do not build up.
    </p>
    <p>
      We may keep information for longer if we must by law, or if it is needed for a complaint or
      legal claim, and only for as long as that requires. Deleted information may remain in our
      providers’ secure backups for a limited period until it is overwritten in their normal backup
      cycle. Resend, for example, deletes data within 90 days of an account being closed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="rights">
    <h2>8. Your rights</h2>
    <p>Under data protection law you have the right to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Access</strong> your personal information and receive a copy of it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rectification</strong>: have inaccurate information corrected or incomplete
        information completed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Erasure</strong>: have your information deleted, for example where we no longer need
        it or you object and we have no overriding reason to keep it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Restriction</strong>: ask us to limit how we use your information, for example while
        we check its accuracy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Object</strong> to our use of your information where we rely on legitimate
        interests. We will stop unless we have compelling legitimate grounds that override your
        interests, or we need it for a legal claim. You can always object to direct marketing, and
        we will then stop.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Data portability</strong>: receive information you gave us in a structured, commonly
        used, machine-readable format, or have it sent to someone else, where we use it on the basis
        of a contract and by automated means.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Complain</strong> to us and to the Information Commission (see section 9).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Some rights only apply in certain circumstances, and the law sets some exceptions. If we can’t
      do what you ask, we will explain why.
    </p>
    <h3>How to use your rights</h3>
    <p>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${business.email}`}>{business.email}</a>
      {business.correspondenceAddress && `, write to us at ${business.correspondenceAddress},`} or
      use the <a href="/contact/">contact form</a>. You don’t need to use particular words. It helps
      if you tell us the email address you used so we can find your information.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no charge. We will respond without undue delay and within one month. If your request
      is complex, or you make a number of requests, we can extend this by up to two more months and
      will tell you why within the first month. If we need more information to confirm your
      identity, the time limit starts when we receive it. If we need you to clarify what you are
      asking for, the time limit is paused until you reply. We may charge a reasonable fee, or
      refuse, only if a request is manifestly unfounded or excessive, and we will explain why.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="complaints">
    <h2>9. Complaints</h2>
    <p>
      If you are unhappy with how we have handled your personal information, please tell us first.
      You can complain online by emailing <a href={`mailto:${business.email}`}>{business.email}</a>
      {business.correspondenceAddress && ` or by writing to ${business.correspondenceAddress}`}. We
      will acknowledge your complaint within 30 days, look into it without undue delay, keep you
      updated and tell you the outcome.
    </p>
    <p>
      You also have the right to complain to the Information Commission, the UK’s data protection
      regulator, which is still known as the ICO:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Online:{' '}
        <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" rel="external">
          ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>Helpline: 0303 123 1113</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The ICO usually expects you to raise a complaint with the organisation first, but you don’t
      have to wait for our response before contacting it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cookies">
    <h2>10. Cookies and similar technologies</h2>
    <p>
      This website uses no analytics, advertising, social media or tracking cookies, and loads no
      fonts, scripts or images from third parties. Because the only cookie we set is strictly
      necessary, the law does not require us to ask for your consent, so there is no cookie banner.
    </p>
    <div class="legal-table" role="region" aria-label="Cookies used" tabindex="0">
      <table role="table">
        <thead role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">
              Cookie
            </th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">
              Purpose
            </th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">
              Lasts
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              <code>presence_receipt</code>
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Purpose">
              Set only after your request has been saved, so the confirmation page can show that it
              was received. It contains a random reference and a security signature, not your
              details. It can only be read by our website, only on the confirmation page, and not by
              scripts in your browser.
            </td>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Lasts">
              10 minutes
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Our hosting provider, Cloudflare, may also set strictly necessary security cookies to protect
      the website from automated attacks. These do not track you across other websites.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can block or delete cookies in your browser settings. If you block the confirmation
      cookie, your request will still be sent, but the confirmation page will show a general message
      instead. If we ever want to use cookies that are not strictly necessary, we will update this
      policy first and ask for your consent where the law requires it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="security">
    <h2>11. How we protect your information</h2>
    <p>We use technical and organisational measures appropriate to the risk, including:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>encrypted connections (HTTPS) for the whole website;</li>
      <li>
        storing enquiries in a database that only we can read, using a secret key or our own
        password-protected hosting account, with no public way to view them;
      </li>
      <li>limiting what we collect, and deleting it on a fixed schedule;</li>
